**Title**  
Expose the original class name as a crew identifier on `CrewBase`‑decorated classes  

**Problem**  
When a class is wrapped by the crew base decorator, there is no straightforward way to retrieve the logical name of the crew it represents. This hampers logging, debugging, and any feature that needs to display or compare crew identities.

**Root Cause**  
The decorator creates a wrapper class but does not propagate the original class’s name as a searchable attribute.

**Fix / Expected Behavior**  
- The wrapper now carries an attribute that stores the original class’s name.  
- The attribute is set automatically for every class decorated with `CrewBase`.  
- Consumers can query this attribute to obtain a human‑readable crew identifier.  
- Existing behavior of the wrapped class remains unchanged aside from the added attribute.  

**Risk & Validation**  
- Adding a new attribute could clash with user‑defined attributes of the same name; review naming conventions to avoid collisions.  
- Verify that all existing unit tests pass and add a test confirming the presence and correctness of the new attribute.  
- Perform integration checks to ensure that downstream components correctly read the new identifier without side effects."},{"id":"ev_exact_source","text":"Current source excerpts in module_code are complete selected AST declarations. Unshown code is not evidence of global absence. Read-only dependencies below are current-source context. Source provenance is stored outside policy messages."}],"history":[],"module_code":{"agent_loop/crew_base.py":"def CrewBase(cls: T) -> T:
